A||||15||MooCow||Fiero and the CRX questions||||11||
Z||000000||MooCow||03-22-2000||06:17 PM||||Okay I will addmitt that I have been debating on getting a CRX instead of a Fiero only at times.  This is what these people on this CRX message board has to say about the Fiero.   Let me know what you all think and is any of it true or is it BS?<P>When GM took the front cradle off of the Citation to create the rear end of the Fiero, they goofed and reduced the engine oil capacity to 3 qts. Add that to the "Iron Duke" engine which had a bad tendency to leak oil from the seals, and you had cars running on 2 qts. of oil, throwing pistons and burning up. GM "fixed" the problem on the 4-cylinder cars by adding an auxiliary 1 qt. oil tank, and by promising free oil changes for the life of the car. As for the 6-cylinder cars, the front 3 spark plugs were next to impossible to reach (next to the firewall), and the the 2.8 liter engine was almost as big a POS as the 2.5 liter 4-banger. Then there was the wonderful Chevette front end from the parts bin (improved on later models). As usual, GM fixed most of the problems too late to save the car. The '88 ones that looked like Ferrari's (sorta) were actually not bad little cars by comparison, but the '84-'86 ones are rolling examples of why I have bought only Hondas since my first one, a '86 CRX. I also debated buying a Fiero myself, but the shitty quality of my '82 Camaro Berlinetta killed that idea (engine blew a head gasket at 69K- the same 2.8 liter in the V6 Fieros). I don't think that you'll ever see a Fiero with 353K miles like the '88 DX I still own in addition to my '91 Si (150K).<P>Drove one of the first Fiero GTs when they came out (first model with the V-6). Horrible car. Shitty handling, really ill-conceived. I was really hoping the General had gotten it right on this one. I was let down, once again. Instead, I bought a used Honda Civic a couple years later, and have never looked back. History has not been kind to the Fiero, and for very good reason; it was simply a terrible car, built on the cheap with subpar materials, sold as something it could never be,and "designed" by costcutting accountants, not by engineers. As per usual GM practice, the accountants told the designers and engineers what they could do, instead of the other way around.<BR>Truth be told, back then I vastly preferred the looks of the Fiero to Toyota's answer to it, the MR-2. Like I said, history has not been kind to the Fiero. You see one now, and it's just kind of a sad reminder of what could have been. Now, look at how many of those old Mister Twos are still running around. How many Fieros do you see on a daily basis?<P>Just for fun, here's a little game you can play with your buddies. When you come up on a Fiero (any year) from behind, bet your buddy that it'll have at least one headlight in the "UP" position. Bet twenty bucks, and you'll rarely lose. It's called a Sucker Bet, 'cause it's a sure thing for you! <P>Well, I guess I will add my opinion to conversation. Back when the Fiero GT with the V6 came out I was just starting to read Car & Driver. One of the first issues I ever picked up had a three way competition between the Fiero, MR2, and the CRX. The first thing I do whenever I read one of those articles is turn to the stats page to read the 1/4 mile times, skidpad numbers, and slalom info. The Fiero was the best in all of those categories with the REX coming in a close second and the MR2 bringing up the rear. So my first thought was cool the Fiero won the competition. Nope, it wasnt even close. The Fiero came in dead last and the REX won. The REX won because it was built right from the ground up. It was easier to drive, it was more fun to drive, and it was built a lot more solid and reliable. The Rex beat out the MR2 on style and price as well as performance (although that was pretty close).<BR>As I think back on that article I cant help but realize that the guys at C&D really called it right that time.<BR>Matter of fact that article was my first look at the REX and I have loved them ever since. I couldnt even drive back then and when I finally did get my liscence I had an 82 Accord for five years before I found a REX of my own but it was the worth the wait. I love my car and the only thing I want to do with it is make it better. I might own other cars in my life but none of them will ever replace my REX. I have owned it 4 years now and I hope to keep it many more with proper love and care. I think that one day in the not to distant future many more people will start to recognize the REX for the true classic car it is destined to become. It truly is a wonderful car to own drive and work on.<BR> <BR>Sorry so long                              <P>Thanks Ken||||reg||11
Z||000001||stimpy||03-22-2000||07:26 PM||||Well, to be honest, many of the things said are accurate opinions. General Motors did force Pontiac to make some compromises with the design.  The rear plugs on the V6 ain't pretty to get to. Many yahoos do drive around with 1 headlight up.<BR>But the CRX a classic? I don't know about that.<BR>Iguess it comes down to preferences, bt I think a classic has somewhat to do with historical significance.  The Fiero used sme pioneering manufacturing techniques, is one of very few affordable mid engined desgns, and all around, my opinion is that the Fiero has far prettier lines.<BR>Besides that, I can't fit in a CRX.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||03-22-2000||08:13 PM||||Wow! I cant believe that rumors like that about the fiero were made up and going around. Fact is the fiero is an awesome looking sports car that can out accelerate, out brake, and out handles a CRX. I agree with you though on the fact that the Japanese do make very reliable well-built cars that the American manufacturer can’t match even today in quality of construction and reliability. I think the fiero is well built for an American car. I have owned an 87 coupe and now own an 88 formula. I can only say this, you have to go out and test drive a fiero and find out for yourself just how fun these little mid engine cars really are (you will be surprised). Read this article.  <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/topfiero/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/topfiero/fiero.htm</A>   Read this article now. <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/topfiero/crx.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/topfiero/crx.htm</A>  This site also has other really good unbiased articles about other great sports cars you may want to read. Read about how safe the fiero is; it scored the second highest crash test safety rating for the eighties (second to the Volvo). Check it out. <A HREF="http://members.tripod.com/FieroDough/safety.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.tripod.com/FieroDough/safety.htm</A>   The true story behind the fiero fires. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.org/text/autoweek.txt" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.org/text/autoweek.txt</A>   I hope this helps you understand more about this car now.||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||03-22-2000||08:32 PM||||I have owned both cars.  My first new car was a '87 CRX.  It was a fun car, great gas mileage, performed reasonably well... heck, it was a great car to drive on gravel mountain roads at unsafe speeds.  <P>However, after I graduated college, I took a ride in my buddy's 86.5 Fiero GT... <B>WoW</B>... needless to say I was hooked.  This car handled like I always thought a sports car should, it had reasonable amount of power and looked really unique!<P>Needless to say, 3 months after my 1st ride, I purchased my own 86.5 GT.  I drove that car  for 7 years, as my only means of transportation!  Sure there were times that i cursed it, I've had complaints about every car I have owned; my 1st car was a '77 Celica with retreads and faded paint, it was the perfect car for a 17 yo(in 1984), hated it; my 2nd car was a 1980 Corolla SR5 Liftback, it looked sporty but had bad brakes and orange stripes, it was OK; the CRX was front wheel drive, hated it; my 91 RX-7 had no torque, hated it; my 98 Jeep Cherokee (current daily driver) is convient for everyone I know, it's ok.<P>In those 7 years of driving, I have replaced one water pump, 2 serpentine belts, 2 clutch's (getrag 5 speed), 2 heater cores, 2 batteries, and 3 sets of rubber.  The car was broken into once and a redneck in a pickup cut in front of me once... each resulting in some sort of body work and paint job.  Lastly, in 1996, the tranny up and broke on me!  Crack, pow, kaBoom!  I then gave my car to my buddy as a parts car.<P>What do you think?  Pretty ugly, huh?  I bet now your believeing all those stories about these cars being a bunch of trash? <P>Well guess what I have had in my garage since last summer?... My GT!  Not a different one, but the same car that I fell in love with 10 years ago!  Am I sick?  Probably.  At least everyone, but my buddy and my girlfriend, thinks so!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  However, as of tuesday I have a tranny for the car!  I'm going to rebuild this car, hopefully better than the original... who knows.<P>So, what's my point?  Good f'n question!  I wanted to show you that I have gone "both ways" with the fiero vs rice car thing, and I truely believe that the best way for <B>you</B> to make a decision for yourself, is to drive as many of each as you can.  See what you think, imagine yourself in each of them, sleep in 'em if you can.  But absolutely, positively, don't buy a car just because your buddy says the other sucks, or you read negative comments from a biased source!!  Whether it's a ricer page, or consumer reports!<P>May the Fiero be with you!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 03-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||03-22-2000||08:37 PM||||OK I dont know much about the Iron Ducks, but the 2.8 motor I really like.<BR>I have worked on peoples CRX's, and ill work on a v6 Fiero any day of the week before working on a CRX.  <BR>As far as a CRX being a better performing car, I dont know the specs all that well, the CRX's I drove didnt even compare to my 85 v6.  Criticizing the handling of the Fiero is silly since it is a different beast.  A mid engine car handles differently than a front engine car.  I could take corners much better in my Fiero when I first got it than in the CRX's I drove.<BR>I guess im a grump, but from my experiences I would never get a CRX.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||03-22-2000||08:45 PM||||In the past 15 years, I have owned a 59 VW, a 73 Rs looking camaro, a 280z, two 280Zx's, a 68 LWB chevy pickup, an 84 Z-28, an 88 CRX Si, a 90 Civic "Econo" hatchback, an 86 Olds Cutlass, currently a Surburban, a 90 Civic Si hatchback and my 2 Fieros. I liked all those vehicles for different reasons. The point? To each his own.||||reg||1
Z||000006||gixxer||03-22-2000||08:50 PM||||A good post Moo Moo Cow. That is the general impression that I have gotten from other sources. I have an 85 CRX Si (the original go-kart), and a '97 Civic Coupe with sway bars and fat tires. (no, it's not lowered, that's why it corners so well)<BR>I intend to sell the CRX this summer and buy a Fiero. Not necessarily in that order.<BR>Here's why; <BR>I don't care what the Fiero was or is, I only care about what it COULD be. <BR>And I think it could be made into a helleva good, go really, really fast second car. It is my opinion that I can go a LOT quicker, more reliably, on a twisty back road, than a modified CRX. <BR>Oh yeah, in Wash, they don't do a visual smog check, just a tailpipe sniffer.=)<BR>The Fiero is a jewel in the rough, but hey, a little (lot) of money in the right places ought to polish it right up. <BR>As a matter of fact, I've been searching the want ads now. So if anybody knows of a good Muncie trans Fiero in the Puget Sound area of Washington state, let me know!<BR>Thanx for reading.<BR>Gixxer<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000007||fierospeeder||03-22-2000||11:10 PM||||I own a 74 beetle, and its one of those kewl cars like a fiero.  I will never buy a jap car only certain european models, but mostly used american cars.  I have the 2.5.  Most of us know the reason of the engine fires, owners lack of care, defective rods.  The car had 4 quarts in it and im pretty sure they just changed the oil filter to an extra 1 qt capacity making it 5 quarts of oil and a different dipstick. Had the recall done by previous owner.  Blown headgasket?  Any car can get one of those.  I had a friend who had a nissan and he blew one, after the mechanic fixed it, he had another one.  You cant judge how realiable a fiero is or how it handles based on you driving a used car from someone.  You never know who owned the car and anything can go bad on a 16 year old car.  Fieros are kewl cars.  What car can take different engine swaps, and have numerous different body swaps. Vw beetles were able to have different engine swaps, and had body kits also.  The only thing you can do with a honda, is add a turbo, ignition mods, and get a 4 cylinder upgraded to a stroker 4 cylinder?  Oh ya and a 5 incher exhaust tip.  My fiero will always look better then a honda.  Most hondas on the road are pretty new and i bet most of them are just a few years, i wonder why theres no break downs.  The 2.5 and the 2.8 have been around for so many years, go to a junkyard and you can see, they were in many, many cars.  One good thing is you will always find a part.  Another thing is, fixing something on the fiero is way easier then a jap car.  Most jap cars have over head cams with timing belts, and because its front wheel, it takes many hours just to get to them.  I had a woman ask me to change hers on a eagle talon, i looked at a repair book and said uh no thankyou.  <P>Just my opinions and hondas can kiss my 2inch exhaust pipe, its proportional to my output.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dan86gt3.8||03-23-2000||12:42 AM||||dude what are they thinking, a crx handling better than a fiero.  I took the secreatary's out one and it squeled the tires when I took a turn at more than 5 mph.  and I redlined it and dropped the clutch and all it did was chirp the tires.  As for performance, any crx I have ever lined up with i have toasted, and I don't even think I have raced one sinse my new motor.  But I doubt one could even hang with me unless it had a big fat turbo.  Anyways tell the crx guys I will drag or roadrace them any day.  As for the relibility of the fiero it was great.  I tend to be hard on all my cars and the fiero has stood up.  All I ever replaced on my fiero was the water pump on the original motor, and the heater core.  But hey I have also had three cars and all 3 had to have water pumps, so hey nothing out of the ordinay and a heater core that goes out after 15 years, hey thats right on schedule.  Anways, the fiero (at least my gt) sticks like glue in the corners and is a lot better powered both sock and not stock than the crx I drove.  Butt then again I didn't drive one with 10,000 stickers, slammed, with a coffe can.  I just drove the base line, but hey I am sure a crx with color coded spark plug wires and a cold air tube will eat anybody's lunch, so hey its your decesion, but I would go with the fiero hands down.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Standard||03-23-2000||01:01 AM||||I kinda lost my faith in the guy that wrote that when he said GM fixed the duke by "adding an auxiliary 1 qt. oil tank"... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  for the record, they used a bigger oil filter.<P>honda badge and front wheel drive do not a sports car make.  imho<P>a stock Fiero in good condition will always handle better than a CRX in good condition.  It's just physics.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||13
Z||000010||Shiner||03-23-2000||02:10 AM||||I agree.  FWD cars will NEVER be sports cars, I don't care what kind of turbo you put on them.  I have heard CRX's handle like total crap.  They don't have fully independent suspension, ya know.  And as for acceleration, well, CRX's should have stickers that say "Powered by a hampster"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||03-23-2000||02:59 AM||||CRX's have a good ole' solid rear axle.. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||87ECO5||03-23-2000||07:08 AM||||The CRX is a very good car.  It's:  reliable, put together well, attractive, quick, fun to drive ... most of the same qualities we look for in our Fieros.<P>Handling - the fact is, even with the new technologies they're applying today (YAW control, etc.), a FWD car will not handle as well as a mid-engined car.<P>"POS" 2.5 - no, the Duke is not one of GM's best pieces of work, and yes, the CRX will outrun it.  Hell, my grandmother can outrun it on a good day.  But, it's a good, reliable engine, it's a common engine (common = less expensive/easy to find parts), and it's VERY easy to work on.  Isn't that what people that buy 4 cylinders want?  No one in their right mind can expect a 4 cylinder to be fast, can they!?<P>Though the Fiero did get a raw deal, I think GM/Pontiac should be commended for some of their innovative ideas.  Recycling items from the parts bin to keep costs down was a good idea (even if we did end up with a Chevette suspension).  Incorporating a space frame which ultimately made the Fiero the second safest car in the world (after Volvo) was brilliant!  And, I've said it before and I'll say it again ... plastic body panels RULE!  I wish Honda, Toyota, Nissan (Datsun) and Mazda had used them on their two-seaters.  If they had, I might be driving one instead of my Fiero.  There's nothing more invigorating than passing a 15-year-old block of Swiss cheese on wheels with my 15-year-old Fiero - with the panels still intact!!!!!<P>Every car has it's positives and negatives.  Like what others said, it boils down to personal preference.  Drive both - drive as many of both as you possibly can - then decide.<P>And, if you end up buying the CRX, then you've made a good choice (because it was your choice - not someone else's).<P>But, we better never see your face here again!!!!!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   just kidding<P>Good luck with your decision ...||||reg||1
Z||000013||1FST2M6||03-23-2000||08:51 AM||||My $.02.. at the shop I deal with there are MANY CRXs at different stages of either disrepair or stupid fast. One of the most popular upgrades in this car is to yank the motor and replace it with a ZC (Jap Spec DOHC motor from 100hp to 140hp) There are about 6 around my area. 2 with nitrous. They can be seen at <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>   I’ve driven them all! They are fun to drive but I can push them through ANY corner I can out accelerate them even with their nitrous running... They just don’t hookup. I can pull an easy 60’ on one before he’s left the light. They are great and like any other car will break down if not properly taken care of. As for the 2.8 being a “crappy” engine. Well I’ve got 96k mile on my clock running Nitrous, a 4 puck racing clutch, sticky tires and I’ll be honest, I beat the hell out of the car. The only thing that has ever gone wrong with my car.. was self inflicted. (I’ve cooked 4 clutches.. the Nitrous doesn’t like stock clutches and sticky tires) Just make sure you drive both cars in the same manner you would everyday, REALLY hard stop light racer or for a commuter and decide then.. don’t let others sway you either way.. you’ll just end up upset cause you REALLY wanted the other car.. The CRX is a great car so is the Fiero. The person that posted in the CRX forum you showed us is just bitter cause he didn’t buy a Fiero in the first place…  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||DRH||03-23-2000||01:44 PM||||"Chevette front end from the parts bin" - interesting comment from a re-bodied Civic driver.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Ken, If the car is 10+ years old I think the amount of repairs you have to do will depend more on how it was taken care of than whether it is a Honda or a GM. They are very different cars with very different advantages and disadvantages. Try to drive more than one example of each and make up your own mind. <P>Good luck, David||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ed||03-23-2000||08:36 PM||||See my related comments on Fieros versus whatevers in "...Wildflowers..." ||||reg||1
